ROCR-Runtime (optional, NOT applied by the main Dockerfile -- see WHY):
report double the real AQL ring size to KFD for GFX7/8 compute queues, so
`hsa_queue_create` can actually get the sizes it asks for instead of
silently degrading to the 64-packet floor.

REQUIRES A PAIRED, OUT-OF-TREE KERNEL PATCH. Without it, this patch alone
produces a real GPU hardware fault under load (`Memory access fault...
Page not present`, `HW Exception ... reason: GPU Hang`) -- confirmed on
real hardware, not theoretical. See the "Kernel-side companion" section
below for exactly what that patch is and why it's not shipped in this
repo. This is why this patch is optional and unwired: this repo's build
pipeline produces a Docker image that runs against whatever kernel the
host already has, and this repo does not build/ship kernel modules (see
AGENTS.md's standing rule -- driver changes belong outside this repo).
Apply this patch, and its kernel companion, together, by hand, only if
you specifically want gfx803's larger queue and are willing to maintain
an out-of-tree kernel module for it.

WHY
---
gfx803's `hsa_queue_create` fails with `HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES`
for every requested queue size except the hard floor of 64 AQL packets
(`Device::acquireQueue`'s halving retry loop in `rocdevice.cpp` gives up
there). `HSA_AGENT_INFO_QUEUE_MAX_SIZE` on this same hardware reports a
normal 131072 -- the agent isn't the thing capping it.

Root-caused via a standalone `hsa_queue_create` probe plus `strace` and
kernel dynamic-debug tracing (`kfd_queue_acquire_buffers`,
`set_queue_properties_from_user`, `kfd_ioctl_create_queue`,
`pqm_create_queue`) to `kfd_queue_buffer_get()`
(`drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c`): it requires an *exact* size
match between the ring size userspace reports via the `hsa_queue_create`
ioctl and the actual VM-mapped buffer backing it. For GFX7/8 AQL compute
queues specifically, `kfd_queue_acquire_buffers()` computes
`expected_queue_size = PAGE_ALIGN(properties->queue_size / 2)` before that
check -- the kernel's own comment: "AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear
twice their actual size". Since this patched ROCr allocates and reports
the *same* value for both, the halved kernel expectation only
coincidentally matches at exactly 1 page (64 packets, where
`PAGE_ALIGN(size/2)` rounds back up to `size`); above that they diverge
and the exact-match check fails every time.

Fix (userspace half): report `ring_size * 2` to the `hsa_queue_create`
ioctl for GFX7/8 AQL compute queues, so KFD's halving lands back on our
real allocation. The physical `ring_buf_` allocation and all AQL packet
indexing elsewhere in this codebase (`queueMask` etc, `rocvirtual.cpp`)
are untouched -- they keep using the real, unscaled `queue_size_pkts`,
which is correct (this exact queue size already worked correctly for
ordinary dispatch before this patch, confirmed via real-hardware MQD
register inspection in this repo's vllm-gfx803/NOTES.md).

Kernel-side companion (REQUIRED, not included here)
----------------------------------------------------
Reporting the doubled size alone is NOT sufficient and is actively
dangerous: `set_queue_properties_from_user()` (`kfd_chardev.c`) copies the
*raw* (doubled) `args->ring_size` into `q_properties->queue_size` with no
GFX7/8-specific adjustment, and `kfd_mqd_manager_vi.c`'s `update_mqd()`
later reads that same raw value to program the actual hardware
`CP_HQD_PQ_CONTROL` queue-size field and `wptr_mask`. Confirmed on real
hardware: applying only this userspace patch, `hsa_queue_create` succeeds
at full size, but the GPU faults under real dispatch load (`Memory access
fault... Page not present`, `HW Exception ... reason: GPU Hang`) --
hardware wraparound is being programmed from the doubled value while the
real backing buffer and software's own index masking are still sized for
the true (unscaled) capacity.

The companion fix: `kfd_queue_acquire_buffers()` already computes the
correct halved value (`expected_queue_size`) for its own VA-size
validation on GFX7/8 AQL queues -- it just never writes that value back.
Making it do so (`properties->queue_size = expected_queue_size;` inside
the existing GFX7/8+AQL+COMPUTE branch, after the halving computation,
before the `kfd_queue_buffer_get()` validation calls) closes the gap:
`pqm_create_queue()` and all hardware MQD programming downstream then see
the same true, unscaled size real backing memory actually has. Verified
end-to-end on real gfx803 hardware, applied to a custom out-of-tree
`amdgpu.ko` built from Fedora's `kernel-7.1.9-200.fc44` source (see this
repo's vllm-gfx803/NOTES.md for the full out-of-tree build recipe, same one
used for the `pqm_debugfs_mqds` NULL-deref fix): `hsa_queue_create`
succeeds at the full 131072-packet max, and vLLM's HIP-graph-replay
`bench latency` repro completes cleanly and repeatably with the enlarged
queue, no hang, no fault -- a 2048x increase over the 64-packet floor.

This kernel patch is not included in this repo on purpose -- it's a
modification to `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c` in the upstream
`amdgpu`/`amdkfd` driver, not something this repo's Docker-image build can
apply or ship (it builds a userspace ROCm stack against whatever kernel
the host already has; it does not build kernel modules). Worth reporting
upstream to `gitlab.freedesktop.org/drm/amd/-/issues` alongside the
`pqm_debugfs_mqds` NULL-deref bug already documented in this repo (both
are the same class of gap: legacy/GFX7-8-specific code paths that clearly
haven't been exercised by anyone in a long time).

Status: WORKING, verified on real hardware, paired with the (undocumented
here, out-of-tree) kernel patch above. Kept optional/unwired specifically
because the kernel half can't travel with this repo -- applying only this
half is worse than applying neither (it trades a safe, low queue size for
a GPU-fault risk under load). If you want gfx803's larger queue, build and
load the kernel companion first, confirm it with a real dispatch (not just
`hsa_queue_create` succeeding) before trusting this patch in anything that
matters.

Applies to: ROCR-Runtime @ rocm-systems commit release/therock-7.14 (same
pin as hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch, applied on top of it).

WHAT
----
- amd_aql_queue.cpp: report `ring_buf_alloc_bytes_ * 2` (instead of
  `ring_buf_alloc_bytes_`) as the `hsa_queue_create` ioctl's ring-size
  argument, for GFX7/8 agents only. No other file changed.

diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp b/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp
index 62bcd92bfc..d4bbca3f0f 100644
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp
@@ -278,17 +278,35 @@ AqlQueue::AqlQueue(core::SharedQueue* shared_queue, GpuAgent* agent, size_t req_
   // is a KFD queue. The debugger may access the aperture addresses, queue
   // scratch base, and queue type.
 
+  // KFD's kfd_queue_acquire_buffers() halves the reported ring_size before
+  // validating it against the actual VM-mapped buffer for GFX7/8 AQL compute
+  // queues ("AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear twice their actual size",
+  // kfd_queue.c) -- report double our real allocation so that halved value
+  // matches what we actually allocated. The physical ring_buf_ allocation
+  // and all AQL packet indexing (queueMask etc, elsewhere in this codebase)
+  // stay based on the real, unscaled queue_size_pkts; only the size KFD is
+  // told about needs doubling. REQUIRES a paired kernel-side fix (see
+  // graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch's header) -- without it, KFD's own
+  // MQD/HQD hardware programming reads this same doubled value raw for
+  // CP_HQD_PQ_CONTROL/wptr_mask, desyncing hardware wraparound from the
+  // real backing buffer and producing a GPU memory-access fault under load.
+  uint64_t reported_ring_size_bytes = ring_buf_alloc_bytes_;
+  if (agent_->supported_isas()[0]->GetMajorVersion() == 7 ||
+      agent_->supported_isas()[0]->GetMajorVersion() == 8) {
+    reported_ring_size_bytes = ring_buf_alloc_bytes_ * 2;
+  }
+
   hsa_status_t status;
   if (core::Runtime::runtime_singleton_->KfdVersion().supports_exception_debugging) {
     queue_rsrc.ErrorReason = &exception_signal_->signal_.value;
     status =
         agent->driver().CreateQueue(node_id, HSA_QUEUE_COMPUTE_AQL, 100, priority_, 0,
-                                    ring_buf_, ring_buf_alloc_bytes_,
+                                    ring_buf_, reported_ring_size_bytes,
                                     ring_buf_metadata_alloc_bytes_,
                                     queue_event(), queue_rsrc);
   } else {
     status = agent->driver().CreateQueue(node_id, HSA_QUEUE_COMPUTE_AQL, 100, priority_, 0,
-                                         ring_buf_, ring_buf_alloc_bytes_,
+                                         ring_buf_, reported_ring_size_bytes,
                                          ring_buf_metadata_alloc_bytes_,
                                          NULL, queue_rsrc);
   }
